## 📦 Full example (copy‑paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with category icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.0.0/dist/maplib

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that

1. **Creates a MapLibre GL JS v6 map** (the API is the same as v2‑v3, just make sure you load the `6.x` bundle).  
2. **Loads 40 + SVG icons** from your own domain (`https://my‑cdn.example.com/icons/…`).  
3. **Adds a GeoJSON source** that contains ~5 000 point features, each with a `category` property that matches one of the icon file names.  
4. **Draws the points with a Symbol layer** that picks the right icon per feature and automatically hides icons that would otherwise overlap when you are zoomed out.

> **Why this works**
> * `map.loadImage()` works with SVG as long as the browser can render it, so we can load each file once and register it with `map.addImage()`.  
> * The Symbol layer uses an **expression** for `icon-image` (`['get', 'category']`) – the value of the `category` property must be exactly the same as the image name you added (without the file extension).  
> * `icon-optional: true` tells the renderer to *drop* an icon when it would collide with another one, which is the simplest way to avoid “piling up” at low zoom levels.  
> * `icon-allow-overlap: false` (the default) keeps the placement algorithm active, and `icon-ignore-placement: false` makes the algorithm respect other symbols (e.g. labels).  
> * A small `icon-size`‑by‑zoom interpolation keeps the icons readable at all scales.

